From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: jrash <johnrash@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 12:56:02 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 10/16] boot loader: don't delete a boot partition that failed
 to mount

On a laptop, an encrypted system installed into a GPT partition
typed as BFS (DriveSetup's choice for a new partition) never got to the
passphrase prompt: the EFI loader crashed (page fault) right after "no
boot path found".

get_boot_file_system() tries to mount each BFS-typed partition on a
boot device, and deleted one that failed. But platform_get_boot_partitions()
had put that partition into bootPartitions with the node's only list
link while gPartitions still pointed to it, so gPartitions was left
pointing to freed memory, and mount_file_systems() crashed walking it.
The partition is now kept: mount_file_systems() then finds the LUKS2
volume in it.

run-boot-test.sh: boot 7 changes the partition's type to BFS and must
reach the passphrase prompt.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 src/system/boot/loader/vfs.cpp               | 12 ++++++++----
 src/tests/system/luks2/qemu/run-boot-test.sh | 13 +++++++++++++
 2 files changed, 21 insertions(+), 4 deletions(-)

diff --git a/src/system/boot/loader/vfs.cpp b/src/system/boot/loader/vfs.cpp
index c188d4f418..90e5988aea 100644
--- a/src/system/boot/loader/vfs.cpp
+++ b/src/system/boot/loader/vfs.cpp
@@ -734,10 +734,14 @@ get_boot_file_system(stage2_args* args, BootVolume& _bootVolume)
 			Directory *fileSystem;
 			error = partition->Mount(&fileSystem, true);
 			if (error != B_OK) {
-				// this partition doesn't contain any known file system; we
-				// don't need it anymore
-				gPartitions.Remove(partition);
-				delete partition;
+				// No file system the loader knows, but keep the partition:
+				// it can be an encrypted volume (in a partition typed as
+				// BFS) that mount_file_systems() finds later. And it can't
+				// be removed safely: a node has a single list link, which
+				// bootPartitions took over while gPartitions still points to
+				// the node, so removing it from gPartitions breaks that list
+				// and deleting it left gPartitions pointing to freed memory
+				// (the loader crashed in mount_file_systems()).
 				continue;
 			}
 
diff --git a/src/tests/system/luks2/qemu/run-boot-test.sh b/src/tests/system/luks2/qemu/run-boot-test.sh
index e810d7dfac..0de2b890a0 100755
--- a/src/tests/system/luks2/qemu/run-boot-test.sh
+++ b/src/tests/system/luks2/qemu/run-boot-test.sh
@@ -23,6 +23,8 @@
 #  - boot 6: the primary's magic damaged too: the loader must find the
 #    volume by its secondary header.
 # Then the build host decrypts the volume and checks what Haiku wrote.
+#  - boot 7: the partition's GPT type changed to BFS (DriveSetup offers it
+#    for a new partition): the loader must still ask for the passphrase.
 #
 # Usage: run-boot-test.sh <anyboot image> <haiku_loader.efi> <work dir>
 #            <host luks> <bfs_shell> <cryptsetup> [timeout seconds]
@@ -257,5 +259,16 @@ fi
 [ "$(cat marker-back)" = "written at boot 6" ] && ok "marker of boot 6" \
 	|| bad "marker: $(cat marker-back)"
 
+echo "== boot 7: the encrypted partition typed as BFS, as DriveSetup offers"
+# The loader tries to mount a BFS-typed partition on its disk as the boot
+# volume; when that failed, it deleted the partition while a list still
+# pointed to it, and crashed in the scan that looks for encrypted volumes.
+sgdisk -t 2:eb00 disk.img > /dev/null
+boot seventh max
+grep -a -q "luks: locked LUKS2 volume" serial-seventh.log \
+	&& ! grep -a -q "Exception Type" serial-seventh.log \
+	&& ok "the loader found the volume in a BFS-typed partition" \
+	|| bad "the loader didn't find the volume in a BFS-typed partition"
+
 echo "$passed passed, $failed failed"
 [ $failed -eq 0 ]
